## Your queue — FIXED. Do not invent work.

`runs/run-{NN}/queue/{x}.md` holds your rows in order. You take the next row. You do not
self-select, re-prioritize, or add rows. When the queue is empty before {STOP_AT}, post
`STATUS` saying so and stop — an empty queue is a real end, not a cue to generate polish work.

## The wave loop

```
read inbox -> claim next row from queue/{x}.md -> build -> test (name every root explicitly)
  -> commit (explicit pathspec, -F message file, judge by `git log -1` grep of the marker)
  -> write evidence/{x}/<row>-<fresh-name>.* -> post VERIFY -> post STATUS -> repeat
```

Read the inbox at the **top of every row** and **after every VERIFY**. No polling loops, no
timers. Post on state change, not on a schedule.

### Your commit marker — fixed format, not a choice

Every commit you make carries this marker on its **first line**:

```
[run-{NN}][lane-{x}][<row-id>] <subject>
```

That exact string is what mandatory line 1 tells you to grep for in `git log -1`, and it is
what the gate's conformance check reads to prove the gate lane built nothing. A commit without
it did not happen, as far as this run is concerned. Write it into the `-F` message file; do not
type it on the command line.

## Testing — scratch policy is not optional

Every test invocation goes through the wrapper. It makes a fresh basetemp; you do not
hand-roll one. **Single quotes are required** so `$env:SCRATCH_RUN_DIR` reaches the wrapper
unexpanded:

```powershell
{SCRATCH_RUN_PS1} -Command 'python -m pytest --basetemp=$env:SCRATCH_RUN_DIR\pytest {TEST_ROOTS}'
```

Never pass a `--basetemp` or `PYTEST_DEBUG_TEMPROOT` that already exists on disk. Never reuse a
`run-*` dir. "It was empty when I checked" is not an exemption — a rotted basetemp cost 250x.
`npx <tool>` is FORBIDDEN if `<tool>` appears anywhere in `package.json`; use `npm run …` or
`node_modules\.bin\…`.

Judge pytest by the **summary line**. A killed run reports exit 0.

